{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents adding, changing, or removing end-user-observable behavior that no agreed requirement already specifies in a Habeas project to draft requirements and commit them, once agreed under {cite}`requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.agreement` and {cite}`requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.commit`, before writing implementation or test code.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill` has these testable parts:\n- The `habeas-requirements` skill directs agents to draft requirements for end-user-observable behavior that no agreed requirement already specifies. This covers adding, changing, or removing behavior.\n- The skill directs agents to commit those requirements once they are agreed, and to do so before writing implementation or test code.\n- The conditions for agreement and for the commit are delegated to the cited `.agreement` and `.commit` requirements. Their own tests, such as `the_requirements_skill_gates_commit_and_code_on_every_group_being_decided` and `the_requirements_skill_commits_every_group_together_before_code`, cover them. The review criteria say not to reassess cited requirements, so I did not.\n\n**Test mapping.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_commits_agreed_requirements_before_code]` carries the `{test}` reference. It reads the real bundled `SKILL.md` through `bundled_skill`, `step_of` and `workflow_steps`.\n- **Principle assertion.** It asserts the skill contains \"Every end-user-observable result of the code, including errors and input handling, must follow from a requirement the user agreed to\". This is the skill's directive that observable behavior needs an agreed requirement, and it matches the requirement's trigger.\n- **Ordering assertion.** It locates the workflow step containing each of these phrases:\n  - \"Write drafts\" (step 2)\n  - \"start a read-only subagent\" (step 3)\n  - the Gate sentence, \"no implementation or test code, and no commit, until the user has agreed to, rejected, or deferred every group\" (step 4)\n  - \"then commit it, all groups together, before any code\" (step 5)\n  - \"Implement and test\" (step 6)\n- **Ordering strictness.** It then asserts `draft < review && review < gate && gate < commit && commit < implement`. A skill that committed after implementation, skipped drafting, or put the gate after the commit would fail.\n- **Helper reliability.** `step_of` panics if a phrase is missing from every step. `workflow_steps` joins each step's continuation lines, so phrases that wrap across lines in `SKILL.md` still match. I checked the skill text, and each phrase is present in the expected step.\n\n**Other scenarios.** The skip case, where an existing requirement already covers the behavior, and the case where agreed text changes are tested under the sibling requirements `no_change`, `text_change` and `agreed_text`. Those are separate requirements, not obligations of this subject. The subject has no boundary or failure cases beyond the draft, agree, commit, code ordering and the trigger principle, and the test asserts both.\n\nI found no gap and no unmarked relevant test.",
  "references": "1e0b64703fd8aacf8ac33b9353292cbe112133deeae5247a05afe1bc8125c74d",
